Hello! To build an isomorphic or server-side rendered (SSR) application with hydration, using React and Firebase, you can follow these steps. This project will include a CMS with features such as WYSIWYG content, publishing, editing, theme switching, subdomains, and a landing page with specific sections. Here’s a high-level overview:

### 1. Project Setup
First, set up your project using Create React App or a similar tool.

```bash
npx create-react-app isomorphic-cms
cd isomorphic-cms
npm install react-router-dom firebase react-quill
```

### 2. Firebase Setup
Configure Firebase in your project and set up Firestore for storing page data.

```javascript
// firebase.js
import firebase from 'firebase/app';
import 'firebase/firestore';

const firebaseConfig = {
  apiKey: "YOUR_API_KEY",
  authDomain: "YOUR_AUTH_DOMAIN",
  projectId: "YOUR_PROJECT_ID",
  storageBucket: "YOUR_STORAGE_BUCKET",
  messagingSenderId: "YOUR_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID",
  appId: "YOUR_APP_ID"
};

firebase.initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
const db = firebase.firestore();

export { db };
```

### 3. Server-Side Rendering (SSR)
Use a tool like Next.js for SSR. If you prefer to stick with React, you can use `react-isomorphic` or `nextjs` for SSR.

### 4. WYSIWYG Editor
Use `react-quill` for the WYSIWYG editor.

```javascript
import ReactQuill from 'react-quill';

// Example usage
<ReactQuill value={content} onChange={setContent} />;
```

### 5. Publishing and Editing Pages
Create a form for creating and editing pages.

```javascript
// pages/CreatePage.js
import { useState } from 'react';
import { db } from '../firebase';
import ReactQuill from 'react-quill';

function CreatePage() {
  const [title, setTitle] = useState('');
  const [content, setContent] = useState('');
  const [datePublished, setDatePublished] = useState(null);

  const handleSubmit = async (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    const docRef = db.collection('pages').doc();
    await docRef.set({
      title,
      content,
      datePublished
    });
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input type="text" value={title} onChange={(e) => setTitle(e.target.value)} />
      <ReactQuill value={content} onChange={setContent} />
      <input type="date" value={datePublished} onChange={(e) => setDatePublished(e.target.value)} />
      <button type="submit">Publish</button>
    </form>
  );
}

export default CreatePage;
```

### 6. Admin Page
Create a basic admin page for managing themes and subdomains.

```javascript
// pages/AdminPage.js
import { useState } from 'react';
import { db } from '../firebase';

function AdminPage() {
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState('light');
  const [subdomain, setSubdomain] = useState('/pages/');

  const handleThemeChange = (e) => {
    setTheme(e.target.value);
  };

  const handleSubdomainChange = (e) => {
    setSubdomain(e.target.value);
  };

  const saveSettings = async () => {
    await db.collection('settings').doc('siteSettings').set({
      theme,
      subdomain
    });
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={(e) => e.preventDefault()}>
      <select value={theme} onChange={handleThemeChange}>
        <option value="light">Light</option>
        <option value="dark">Dark</option>
      </select>
      <input type="text" value={subdomain} onChange={handleSubdomainChange} />
      <button onClick={saveSettings}>Save</button>
    </form>
  );
}

export default AdminPage;
```

